<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Natives Land Act, 1913</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1913">1913</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Natives Land Act of 1913 was a law of the Union of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a> that barred Africans from buying or renting land outside designated reserves, which then covered about 7 per cent of the country. It ended African sharecropping on white-owned farms in much of the country and drove many families off the land. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sol-plaatje">Sol Plaatje</a> condemned it in Native Life in South Africa (1916), and the reserves were later enlarged under the Native Trust and Land Act of 1936.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Natives Land Act</b> of 1913 confined African land ownership to reserves covering about 7 per cent of the country, raised to about 13 per cent by the Native Trust and Land Act of 1936; the writer Sol Plaatje condemned it in Native Life in South Africa (1916).</blockquote><a href="/empire/union-of-south-africa">Union of South Africa · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
